      "text": "To solve for \\(x + y\\) given the equation \\(x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34\\), we start by rearranging the equation to bring all terms to one side:\n\n\\[x^2 - 10x + y^2 + 6y = -34.\\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for both the \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) terms. For the \\(x\\) terms, \\(x^2 - 10x\\), we add and subtract 25:\n\n\\[x^2 - 10x = (x-5)^2 - 25.\\]\n\nFor the \\(y\\) terms, \\(y^2 + 6y\\), we add and subtract 9:\n\n\\[y^2 + 6y = (y+3)^2 - 9.\\]\n\nSubstituting these completed squares back into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[(x-5)^2 - 25 + (y+3)^2 - 9 = -34.\\]\n\nSimplifying the equation, we have:\n\n\\[(x-5)^2 + (y+3)^2 - 34 = -34.\\]\n\nAdding 34 to both sides, we get:\n\n\\[(x-5)^2 + (y+3)^2 = 0.\\]\n\nSince the sum of two squares is zero, each square must be zero:\n\n\\[(x-5)^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (y+3)^2 = 0.\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[x - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y + 3 = 0.\\]\n\nSo, we find:\n\n\\[x = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = -3.\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum \\(x + y\\) is:\n\n\\[x + y = 5 + (-3) = 2.\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{2}.\\]",
