      "text": "We start with the given equation:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34\n\\]\nFirst, we rearrange the equation to bring all terms to one side:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 - 10x + 6y + 34 = 0\n\\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). Starting with \\(x\\), we take the coefficient of \\(x\\), which is \\(-10\\), divide it by 2 to get \\(-5\\), and then square it to get \\(25\\). For \\(y\\), the coefficient is \\(6\\), so we divide it by 2 to get \\(3\\), and then square it to get \\(9\\).\n\nAdding these values to the equation, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x + 25 + y^2 + 6y + 9 = -34 + 25 + 9\n\\]\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we have:\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x + 25 + y^2 + 6y + 9 = 0\n\\]\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0\n\\]\n\nSince the sum of two squares is zero, each square must individually be zero. Therefore, we have:\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (y + 3)^2 = 0\n\\]\nThis gives us:\n\\[\nx - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y + 3 = 0\n\\]\nSo, we find:\n\\[\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = -3\n\\]\n\nFinally, we calculate \\(x + y\\):\n\\[\nx + y = 5 + (-3) = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(x + y\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
